## 11 : REDIS RAM LIMIT vs TOTAL SERVER RAM
==============================================

Server ki Total RAM aur Redis / Cache ke liye available
memory same cheez nahi hoti.

Suppose ek server ke paas:

    Total Server RAM = 32 GB


Us server par multiple processes run kar rahe hain:

+----------------------------------+
|            SERVER RAM            |
|                                  |
|  OS                  →  4 GB     |
|  Application         →  6 GB     |
|  Database            → 10 GB     |
|  Redis / Cache       →  8 GB     |
|  Other Processes     →  4 GB     |
|                                  |
+----------------------------------+

              Total = 32 GB


Yahan:

    Total Server RAM = 32 GB

Lekin Redis ke paas poori 32 GB RAM available nahi hai.

Redis ke liye configuration ke through ek maximum memory
limit set ki ja sakti hai.

For example:

    Redis Max Memory = 8 GB


To jab hum bolte hain:

    Cache Memory Full

iska matlab zaruri nahi hai ki:

    Entire Server RAM Full


Iska matlab ye ho sakta hai ki Redis apni configured
maximum memory limit tak pahunch gaya hai.

Once Redis reaches its configured maximum memory limit,
new data store karne ke liye configured eviction policy
apply ho sakti hai.

Conceptually:

    Redis Memory Usage
           ↓
    Reaches Max Memory Limit
           ↓
    New Data Needs to Be Stored
           ↓
    Eviction Policy Decides
    Which Existing Entry to Remove
           ↓
    Memory Freed
           ↓
    New Data Stored





========================
## 12 : CACHE EVICTION
========================

Cache Eviction ka meaning hai:

Redis/Cache ki configured RAM limit reach hone par existing cache
entries ko configured eviction policy ke according remove karna,
taaki new data store karne ke liye space available ho sake.


## WHY IS CACHE EVICTION NEEDED?
---------------------------------

TTL expire hone par cache entries automatically remove ho sakti hain.

Lekin ek situation aur ho sakti hai:

TTL expire hone se pehle hi Redis ki configured RAM limit
reach ho jaye.


## Example:

Redis Max Memory = 8 GB

Current Usage = 8 GB


Ab Application ek new entry store karna chahti hai:

SET product:9999 {...}


Lekin Redis ke paas new data store karne ke liye
configured RAM limit ke andar sufficient space available nahi hai.

Is situation me Redis ki configured Eviction Policy decide karti hai
ki existing cache entries me se kaunsi entry remove ki jaye,
taaki new data store karne ke liye space available ho sake.


## GENERIC CACHE EVICTION STRATEGIES
---------------------------------------

### 1. LRU — Least Recently Used
--------------------------------

LRU me woh cache entry remove ki jaati hai
jo sabse zyada time se access nahi hui hai.

Example:

product:101 → Last Accessed: 1 minute ago
product:102 → Last Accessed: 5 minutes ago
product:103 → Last Accessed: 30 minutes ago

Memory full hone par agar `product:104` store karna ho,
to LRU policy `product:103` ko evict kar sakti hai.


### 2. LFU — Least Frequently Used
----------------------------------

LFU me woh cache entry remove ki jaati hai
jise sabse kam number of times access kiya gaya hai.

Example:

product:101 → Access Count = 1000
product:102 → Access Count = 500
product:103 → Access Count = 2

Memory full hone par agar `product:104` store karna ho,
to LFU policy `product:103` ko evict kar sakti hai.


### 3. FIFO — First In First Out
--------------------------------

FIFO me jo cache entry sabse pehle Cache me insert hui thi,
use sabse pehle remove kiya jata hai.

Example:

Insertion Order:

A → B → C

Memory full hone par `D` insert karne ke liye:

A → Evicted

Result:

B → C → D


### 4. RANDOM EVICTION
---------------------

Is approach me Cache randomly kisi existing
cache entry ko remove kar sakta hai.



## REDIS CACHE EVICTION STRATEGIES
---------------------------------------

Ab Redis isi generic cache eviction policies ko apne specific eviction policies ke through implement karta hai. 

Jab Redis configured maxmemory limit reach karta hai, to selected eviction policy ke according keys evict ki ja sakti hain.


                    REDIS EVICTION POLICIES
                              │
          ┌───────────────────┼───────────────────┐
          │                   │                   │
         LRU                 LFU                RANDOM
          │                   │                   │
     ┌────┴────┐         ┌────┴────┐         ┌────┴────┐
     │         │         │         │         │         │
  allkeys   volatile  allkeys   volatile  allkeys   volatile


          ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
          │    ADDITIONAL REDIS POLICIES     │
          └────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                           │
                 ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                 │                   │
            volatile-ttl         noeviction


                         FIFO
                          │
                          ▼
          Not directly supported by Redis


## EXAMPLE:
-------------

Suppose Redis me ye 4 cache entries hain:

product:101 → TTL = 10 min
product:102 → TTL = 20 min
product:103 → No TTL
product:104 → No TTL


Redis ki configured `maxmemory` limit reach ho gayi hai.

Ab ek nayi entry store karni hai:

product:105

Memory free karne ke liye Redis ko existing cache entries me se kisi entry ko evict karna hoga.

Lekin yahan ek important question hai:

Eviction policy kin keys par apply hogi?

Redis keys ko broadly do groups me consider karta hai:


### 1. ALLKEYS
---------------

`allkeys` ka matlab:

Redis ki **saari keys** eviction ke liye eligible hain,
chahe unke saath TTL configured ho ya nahi.

Hamare example me:

Eligible Keys:

product:101
product:102
product:103
product:104


Ab configured eviction algorithm decide karega
ki in eligible keys me se kaunsi key remove karni hai.

Examples:

allkeys-lru
→ All keys me se Least Recently Used key remove karo.

allkeys-lfu
→ All keys me se Least Frequently Used key remove karo.

allkeys-random
→ All keys me se randomly kisi ek key ko remove karo.



### 2. VOLATILE
----------------

`volatile` ka matlab:

Sirf woh keys eviction ke liye eligible hain
jin par TTL configured hai.

Hamare example me:

Eligible Keys:

product:101
product:102


`product:103` aur `product:104` eviction candidates nahi hain,
kyunki inke paas TTL nahi hai.

Ab configured eviction algorithm eligible keys me se
kaunsi key remove hogi, ye decide karega.

Examples:

volatile-lru
→ TTL wali keys me se Least Recently Used key remove karo.

volatile-lfu
→ TTL wali keys me se Least Frequently Used key remove karo.

volatile-random
→ TTL wali keys me se randomly kisi ek key ko remove karo.


### 3. ADDITIONAL REDIS EVICTION POLICIES
------------------------------------------

## 3.1 VOLATILE-TTL
--------------------

`volatile-ttl` policy me sirf TTL configured keys
eviction ke liye eligible hoti hain.

No-TTL keys eviction candidates nahi hoti.

Eligible keys me se Redis generally us key ko evict karne
ki preference deta hai jiski remaining TTL sabse kam hai,
yaani jo sabse jaldi expire hone wali hai.

Example:

product:101 → TTL = 10 min
product:102 → TTL = 20 min

volatile-ttl ke case me remaining TTL matter karegi, original TTL nahi.

Redis `product:101` ko eviction ke liye select kar sakta hai,
kyunki uski remaining TTL comparatively kam hai.


## 3.2 NO EVICTION
--------------------

noeviction policy me Redis, configured maxmemory limit reach hone ke baad existing keys ko automatically evict nahi karta.

Agar new data store karne ke liye additional memory required ho, to Redis write operation ko reject kar deta hai aur error return karta hai.


## IMPORTANT :
---------------

volatile policies me sirf TTL wali keys eviction candidates hoti hain.

Agar Redis memory full hone par sufficient eligible TTL keys
available nahi hain, to Redis required memory free nahi kar
paayega aur write operation fail ho sakta hai.





