## 13 : CACHE WRITE PATTERNS
============================

Abhi tak humne mainly read path dekha.

## EXAMPLE:
-------------

GET /products/101
     ↓
 Controller 
     ↓
  Service
     ↓
   Cache
     ↓
Cache Hit?
 ├── YES → Return Data
 │
 └── NO
      ↓
    Database
      ↓
 Store in Cache
      ↓
 Return Data


Lekin real problem write ke time aati hai.

Suppose currently:

Database:

productId:101
price = ₹79,999

Aur Cache me bhi:

productId:101
price = ₹79,999

Ab product ka price update hota hai:

PUT /products/101

New Price = ₹69,999

Ab question:

Database aur Cache ko kis order me update karein?

Isi problem ko handle karne ke liye different Cache Write Patterns use kiye jaate hain.

-------------------------
1. WRITE-THROUGH CACHE
-------------------------

Write-Through Cache me Database update ke saath
corresponding Cache entry ko bhi latest data se update
kar diya jata hai.

Isliye next read request par:

Cache Hit
    ↓
Latest Data directly Cache se return


Write-Through useful ho sakta hai jab updated data ko
immediately aur frequently read kiya jana expected ho,
kyunki updated data pehle se Cache me available hota hai.


## LIMITATIONS OF WRITE-THROUGH
-------------------------------

Write-Through me Database aur Cache dono ko update karna hota hai,
isliye failure scenarios handle karne padte hain.

### CASE 1: DATABASE UPDATE SUCCESS, CACHE UPDATE FAILS
--------------------------------------------------------

Database successfully update ho gaya, lekin Cache update fail ho gaya.

Is case me Cache purana data return kar sakta hai,
yaani stale data temporarily serve ho sakta hai.

Agar Cache entry ke saath TTL configured hai, to TTL expire hone ke
baad next request Database se latest data fetch karke Cache ko
update kar deti hai.


### CASE 2: CACHE UPDATE SUCCESS, DATABASE UPDATE FAILS
--------------------------------------------------------

Ye comparatively more problematic scenario hai.

Cache me new data store ho gaya, lekin Database update fail ho gaya.

Isliye temporarily users ko Cache se updated data mil sakta hai.

Lekin TTL expire hone ke baad Cache Miss hoga aur Database se
purana data dobara fetch hoke Cache me store ho jayega.

Iske baad subsequent requests me users ko old data milna
shuru ho jayega.

Yaani jo updated data temporarily Cache me available tha,
woh Database me successfully persist nahi hua aur eventually
lost ho gaya.


## IMPORTANT
------------

Isliye practical systems me Database aur Cache ke update order,
failure handling, retry mechanisms, etc. carefully design karne padte hain.

Normal application caching use cases me Cache-Aside + Invalidation
aksar simpler aur commonly preferred approach hoti hai, kyunki
Database source of truth rehta hai aur Cache ko temporary
data ke roop me treat kiya jata hai.


-------------------------------------
2. WRITE-BEHIND / WRITE-BACK CACHE
-------------------------------------

Ab tak humne dekha:

Write-Through
→ Database me data update karne ke saath-saath
  Cache me bhi latest data update kiya jata hai.


Lekin Write-Behind me approach different hoti hai.

Jab data update karne ki request aati hai, to Application
pehle updated data ko Cache me store karti hai aur client ko
success response return kar deti hai.Uske baad Database me data 
ko asynchronously update kiya jata hai.

## NOTE:
---------
Database ko immediately update nahi kiya jata.


## WHY USE WRITE-BEHIND?
-------------------------

Write-Behind ka main benefit ye hai ki client ko Database
update complete hone ka wait nahi karna padta.

Isliye client ko jaldi response mil sakta hai, jisse
response time aur user experience better ho sakta hai.

Ye high write workloads me useful ho sakta hai, kyunki
Database update asynchronously baad me perform kiya ja sakta hai.


## LIMITATIONS OF WRITE-BEHIND
--------------------------------

Write-Behind me Cache update hone ke baad client ko
immediately success response mil sakta hai, jabki Database
update asynchronously later hota hai.

Agar Database update hone se pehle Cache fail ho jaye,
to updated data Database me persist nahi ho payega.

Isliye Write-Behind me data loss ka risk ho sakta hai.


-------------------------
3. WRITE-AROUND CACHE
-------------------------

Ab tak humne dekha:

Write-Through
→ Database update ke saath-saath
  Cache me bhi latest data update kiya jata hai.


Write-Behind / Write-Back
→ Updated data pehle Cache me store kiya jata hai.
→ Database update baad me asynchronously hota hai.



Write-Around ka main idea ye hai ki write request ke case me
new data directly Database me write / update kiya jata hai.

Agar corresponding data pehle se Cache me present hai,
to stale data avoid karne ke liye us Cache entry ko
invalidate / delete kiya jata hai.

Is approach me mainly write path me Cache ko bypass kiya jata hai.
Yaani updated data ko directly Cache me populate nahi kiya jata.

Ab same data ke liye next read request par Cache Miss hoga.

Is case me latest data Database se fetch karke Cache me
store kiya ja sakta hai aur response return kiya jata hai.


## WHY USE WRITE-AROUND CACHE ?
--------------------------------

Write-Around Cache useful ho sakta hai jab data frequently
update hota ho, lekin frequently read nahi hota.

Aise case me har write ke baad Cache ko update karna
unnecessary ho sakta hai.


## LIMITATIONS OF WRITE-AROUND
--------------------------------

Write operation ke baad updated data Cache me available nahi hota,
kyunki write ke time corresponding Cache entry invalidate/delete
kar di gayi thi.

Isliye subsequent read request par Cache Miss hota hai.

Application ko latest data Database se fetch karna padta hai,
jo Cache se data retrieve karne ke comparison me relatively
slower ho sakta hai.

Isliye write ke baad first read comparatively slower ho sakti hai.





===========================================
## 14 : CACHE STAMPEDE / THUNDERING HERD
===========================================

Abhi tak humne dekha ki agar requested data Cache me available hai, to application directly Cache se data return kar deti hai. 
Lekin agar requested data Cache me available nahi hai, yani Cache Miss hota hai, to application Database se data fetch karti hai,
use Cache me store karti hai, aur phir response return karti hai.

Normally ye flow perfectly fine hai.

-----------------------------
## CACHE STAMPEDE KYA HAI?
-----------------------------

Cache Stampede, jise Thundering Herd bhi kaha jata hai, tab hota hai jab koi popular cached data 
unavailable ho jata hai aur uske turant baad usi data ke liye bahut saari concurrent requests 
ek saath aati hain. Ye requests Cache Miss encounter karti hain aur same data 
ko fetch karne ke liye ek saath Database ya kisi backend system ko hit karne lagti hain.

Suppose ek popular product cache me stored hai:

    Key:
        product:101

    Value:
        {
            "id": 101,
            "name": "iPhone",
            "price": 79999
        }

TTL = 10 minutes

TTL expire hone ke baad `product:101` Cache se remove ho jata hai.

Exactly usi time Product:101 ke liye 10,000 concurrent
requests aa jaati hain.

Sab requests Cache check karti hain aur unhe:

    Cache Miss

milta hai.

Ab ye saari 10,000 concurrent requests independently same product data ke liye Database ya backend system ko hit karne lagti hain.
Isse Database par ek sudden load spike aa sakta hai, jiske kaaran Database overload ho sakta hai, response time increase ho sakta hai, 
aur overall application performance impact ho sakti hai.


----------------------------------
## REASONS BEHIND CACHE STAMPEDE
----------------------------------

Popular cache entry unavailable hone ke common reasons:

##1. TTL EXPIRY
----------------

   Popular cache key ka TTL expire ho jata hai.

##2. CACHE EVICTION
--------------------

   Memory pressure ke kaaran eviction policy popular cache entry
   ko remove kar sakti hai.

##3. CACHE INVALIDATION
------------------------

   Data update hone par popular cache entry invalidate ya delete
   ki ja sakti hai.

##4. CACHE RESTART OR FAILURE
------------------------------

   Cache server restart, fail ya temporarily unavailable ho sakta hai.

## 5. CACHE FLUSH
-------------------

   Cache ki multiple ya saari entries remove ho sakti hain.


## CACHE STAMPEDE SOLUTIONS :
------------------------------

→ Locking / Request Coalescing

→ Stale-While-Revalidate / Serve Stale While Refreshing


---------------------------------
## LOCKING / REQUEST COALESCING  
---------------------------------

## CORE IDEA
-------------

Single Cache Entry Expiration ki wajah se hone wale Cache Stampede ko prevent karne ka core idea ye hai ki same cache key ke liye 
multiple concurrent Cache Misses hone par sab requests independently Database ko hit na karein.
Ideally, sirf ek request Database se data fetch karke Cache ko populate kare, aur baaki requests us result ka wait karein. 
Cache populate hone ke baad, woh requests Cache se data retrieve kar sakein.


#1 : SINGLE SERVICE INSTANCE — LOCAL LOCK
-------------------------------------------

Agar Product Service ka sirf ek instance hai aur sab requests isi instance par aa rahi hain, to Cache Stampede handle karne ke liye local lock use kiya ja sakta hai.

Java me ise synchronized block / method ya kisi similar locking mechanism se implement kar sakte hain.


        Multiple Requests
               │
       ┌───────┼────────┐
       ↓       ↓        ↓
      R1      R2       R3
       │       │        │
       └───────┼────────┘
               ↓
         Product Service
               ↓
          Local Lock

Har request sabse pehle Redis Cache ko check karegi.

Agar requested data Cache me available hai, to request directly data return kar degi aur lock ki koi zarurat nahi hogi.

Lekin agar Cache Miss hota hai, to request synchronized block me enter karne ke liye local lock acquire karne ki koshish karegi.

Ek time par sirf ek thread synchronized block ke andar execute kar sakta hai. Baaki threads lock available hone tak wait karte hain.

Jo thread pehle lock acquire karta hai, wo Cache ko dobara check karta hai. Agar data ab bhi Cache me available nahi hai, to wo Database se data fetch karke Redis Cache me store kar deta hai.

Uske baad lock release ho jata hai. Waiting threads ek-ek karke lock acquire karti hain aur Cache ko dobara check karti hain. 

Kyunki pehli request already data Cache me store kar chuki hoti hai, unhe Database ko dobara hit karne ki zarurat nahi padti.


## Conceptually:
------------------

// First Cache Check
data = redis.get(key);

if (data != null) {
    return data;
}

synchronized (lock) {

    // Check Cache Again
    data = redis.get(key);

    if (data != null) {
        return data;
    }

    // Fetch from Database
    data = database.get(key);

    // Store in Cache
    redis.set(key, data);

    return data;
}


## WHY CACHE KO DOBARA CHECK KARTE HAIN SYNCHRONISED BLOCK KE AANDAR ?
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose R1, R2 aur R3 tino requests ko initially Cache Miss milta hai.

Synchronized block ke bahar wala first Cache check har incoming request ke liye hota hai. Iska purpose ye check karna hai ki requested data already Cache me available hai ya nahi. 
Agar Cache Hit mil jata hai, to request directly data return kar deti hai aur lock acquire karne ki zarurat nahi padti.

Lekin agar Cache Miss hota hai, to request synchronized block ka lock acquire karne ki koshish karti hai.

R1 pehle local lock acquire kar leta hai, jabki R2 aur R3 lock available hone ka wait karte hain.

R1 Database se data fetch karke Redis Cache me store kar deta hai aur synchronized block se exit karte hi lock release ho jata hai.

Ab jab R2 lock acquire karta hai, tab synchronized block ke andar wala second Cache check important hota hai. R2 jab pehli baar Cache check kar rahi thi, tab data available nahi tha. Lekin lock ka wait karne ke dauran R1 Database se data fetch karke Cache populate kar chuki hoti hai.

Isliye R2 dobara Cache check karti hai aur use data mil jata hai. Database hit karne ki zarurat nahi padti. Same process R3 ke saath bhi hota hai.

Isi second Cache check ki wajah se lock ke liye wait kar rahi multiple requests same data ke liye baar-baar Database ko hit karne se bachti hain.



#2 : MULTIPLE SERVICE INSTANCES — DISTRIBUTED LOCK
--------------------------------------------------

Product Service horizontally scaled hai aur multiple instances
par run kar rahi hai. In instances ke beech requests distribute
karne ke liye ek Service Load Balancer configured hai.

Architecture conceptually kuch aisa hai:

                   Load Balancer
                        |
    +-------------+--------------------------+-------------+          
    |             |             |            |             |  
    v             v             v            v             v 
    P1            P2            P3           P4            P5 
    |             |             |            |             |  
    +-------------+-------------+------------+-------------+
                        |
                        v
                      Redis
                        |
                        v
                    Product DB

Load Balancer incoming requests ko different Product Service
instances ke across distribute karega.

Har request eventually kisi na kisi Product Service instance
tak pahunchti hai, aur woh instance pehle Redis Cache check
karti hai.


