### IMPORTANT POINT
---------------------

Local / In-Memory Cache Redis ka replacement nahi hai.

Ye Redis ke saath ek additional caching layer ke roop me
use kiya ja sakta hai.


### LOCAL CACHE KA MAIN BENEFIT
--------------------------------

Distributed Systems me Redis generally ek external Distributed Cache
ki tarah use hota hai. Isliye Application ko Redis access karne ke liye
network call karni padti hai.

Even though Redis bahut fast hota hai, phir bhi Redis ko access karne me
network communication ka overhead hota hai.

Frequently accessed data ko Local Cache me store karne par, kai
requests Redis tak pahunchne se pehle hi Application Instance ki
Local Cache se serve ho sakti hain.

Isse Redis par overall request traffic reduce hota hai. Redis ko kam
requests jaane ki wajah se network calls bhi reduce hoti hain, jisse
overall response latency reduce ho sakti hai.

Benefits:

    ↓ Redis Traffic
    ↓ Network Calls
    ↓ Response Latency


### LOCAL CACHE KA PROBLEM
-----------------------------

Distributed System me multiple Application Instances ho sakte hain aur 
har Application Instance apni separate Local Cache maintain karta hai.
Yaani, ye Local Caches ek dusre ke saath shared nahi hote.


Maan lo, initially `product:101` ka current data Database, Shared Redis Cache
aur sabhi Application Instances ki Local Caches me consistent hai:

Database               → product:101 → Price = ₹100

Shared Redis Cache     → product:101 → Price = ₹100

Instance 1 Local Cache → product:101 → Price = ₹100
Instance 2 Local Cache → product:101 → Price = ₹100
Instance 3 Local Cache → product:101 → Price = ₹100


Ab maan lo product:101 ka price update karne ke liye ek write request Instance 1 ke paas aati hai:

Price: ₹100 → ₹120

Instance 1:

1. Database update karti hai
        ↓
2. Shared Redis Cache ko update / invalidate karti hai
        ↓
3. Apni Local Cache ko invalidate karti hai


### UPDATE KE BAAD CURRENT STATE:
----------------------------------

Database → product:101 → Price = ₹120

Redis → product:101 → Updated / Deleted

Instance 1 Local Cache → Deleted
Instance 2 Local Cache → Price = ₹100
Instance 3 Local Cache → Price = ₹100

Ab Instance 1 ki Local Cache invalidate ho gayi hai, lekin Instance 2
aur Instance 3 ki Local Caches me purana data abhi bhi stored hai.

Agar subsequent request Instance 2 ke paas jaati hai:

Request
   ↓
Instance 2 / 3
   ↓
Local Cache → HIT
   ↓
Price = ₹100

To stale data return ho jayega.


### REASON :
------------
Har Application Instance apni separate Local Cache maintain karti hai.
Isliye ek instance ki Local Cache invalidate hone se baaki instances ki Local Caches automatically invalidate nahi hoti.


### IS PROBLEM KO HANDLE KAISE KARTE HAIN?
-------------------------------------------

##1. SHORT TTL
-----------------

Distributed System me Application Instances ki Local Cache entries ke
saath relatively short TTL configure ki ja sakti hai.

## EXAMPLE :
--------------

product:101

Local Cache TTL = 30 seconds

Agar Database aur Shared Cache (e.g. Redis) update ho jaye, lekin kisi
reason se Local Cache invalidate na ho, to stale data permanently Local
Cache me nahi rahega.

TTL expire hone ke baad:

Local Cache → MISS
       ↓
Redis → Latest Data
       ↓
Populate Local Cache
       ↓
Return Latest Data

TTL ek safety mechanism ki tarah kaam karta hai, lekin TTL expire
hone tak stale data serve ho sakta hai.


##2. EVENT-BASED CACHE INVALIDATION
-------------------------------------

Abhi humne dekha ki Distributed System me har Application Instance apni
separate Local / In-Memory Cache maintain karti hai.

Write request ke case me Database update karna aur Shared Redis Cache
ko invalidate/update karna relatively straightforward hai.

Lekin Local Cache ke saath problem ye hai ki writing request jis
Application Instance ke paas aati hai, woh maximum apni Local Cache
ko invalidate kar sakti hai.

Woh directly doosre Application Instances ki Local Caches ko
invalidate nahi kar sakti.

Isliye agar data Database me update ho jaye aur Redis bhi
invalidate/update ho jaye, tab bhi doosre Application Instances ki
Local Caches me purana data stored reh sakta hai.

Agar subsequent request kisi aise instance ke paas jaati hai jahan
Local Cache me purana data available hai, to stale data serve ho sakta hai.

Is problem ko solve karne ke liye hume aisa mechanism chahiye jisse
data update hone par sabhi Application Instances ko notify kiya ja sake
ki corresponding Local Cache entry invalidate karni hai.

Isi approach ko Event-Based Cache Invalidation kehte hain.


## FLOW :
----------

Maan lo `product:101` ko update karne ke liye write request
Application Instance 1 ke paas aati hai.

Application Instance 1 Database update karti hai aur corresponding
Redis Cache entry ko update ya invalidate karti hai.

Uske baad Application ek Cache Invalidation Event publish karti hai:

CacheInvalidationEvent : key = product:101

Ye event aise messaging mechanism ke through sabhi relevant Application Instances tak deliver kiya jata hai, 
jisse har instance corresponding Local Cache entry invalidate kar sake.

Har Application Instance received key `product:101` ko apni Local
Cache se invalidate kar deti hai.

Ab kisi bhi instance par next request aane par Local Cache me stale
data nahi milega. Application Redis se latest data fetch karke
response return karegi.

Is tarah Event-Based Cache Invalidation ke through Distributed Systems me 
multiple Application Instances ki Local Caches ko invalidate kiya ja sakta hai.


## LIMITATION: EVENT DELIVERY FAILURE
-------------------------------------

Event-Based Cache Invalidation me data update hone ke baad ek
Cache Invalidation Event relevant Application Instances ko bheja
jata hai taaki woh corresponding Local Cache entry invalidate kar saken.

Lekin possible hai ki kisi instance ko event receive na ho.

Maan lo product:101 update hua:

Database Updated
       ↓
Redis Updated / Invalidated
       ↓
Cache Invalidation Event Published
       ↓

Event Instance 1 aur Instance 2 tak successfully pahunch gaya:

Instance 1 → Event Received → Local Cache Invalidated

Instance 2 → Event Received → Local Cache Invalidated

Lekin kisi reason se Instance 3 event receive nahi kar paya:

Instance 3 → Event Not Received
     
Isliye Instance 3 ki Local Cache me purana data abhi bhi stored reh
sakta hai:

Agar subsequent request Instance 3 ke paas jaati hai:

Request
   ↓
Local Cache → HIT
   ↓
Stale Data


## TTL AS A SAFETY NET
-----------------------

Agar kisi Application Instance ko Cache Invalidation Event miss ho jaye,
to stale data uski Local Cache me indefinitely nahi rehna chahiye.

Isliye Local Cache entries ke saath TTL configure kiya ja sakta hai.

Event-Based Invalidation + TTL Safety Net → Prevents Stale Data from Remaining Indefinitely


TTL expire hone ke baad:

Local Cache Entry Expires
          ↓
Next Request → Local Cache MISS
          ↓
Fetch Latest Data from Shared Cache (e.g. Redis)
          ↓
Populate Local Cache



##2. READ REPLICAS / REPLICA-BASED READ DISTRIBUTION
------------------------------------------------------

Redis Cluster me different cache keys different Redis Nodes par
distributed ho sakti hain.

Maan lo `product:101` ek Hot Key ban jaati hai aur iske liye
extremely high number of requests aa rahi hain.


Example:

product:101 → Node 1 -> 1M req/sec 🔥
product:102 → Node 2 -> 100 req/sec  
product:103 → Node 3 -> 120 req/sec  


Yaani Redis Cluster me multiple Nodes hone ke bawajood, ek particular
Hot Key ka traffic usi Node par concentrate ho sakta hai jahan woh
key stored hai.

Result:

Node 1 → High Load
Node 2 → Low Load
Node 3 → Low Load

