## APPROACH 1 : UPDATE ALL CACHE COPIES
------------------------------------------

Is approach me Database successfully update hone ke baad Application
sabhi Hot Key cache copies ko latest data ke saath update karti hai.

### CURRENT STATE
-------------------
Database:

product:101 → {iPhone, ₹120}

Redis:

product:101:1 → {iPhone, ₹100}
product:101:2 → {iPhone, ₹100}
product:101:3 → {iPhone, ₹100}

Application ko pata hai ki N = 3 copies maintain ki ja rahi hain.

Application `1` se `N` tak har cache key ko latest data ke saath
update karne ki request bhejti hai:

SET product:101:1 {iPhone, ₹120}
SET product:101:2 {iPhone, ₹120}
SET product:101:3 {iPhone, ₹120}

Redis Client har key ka hash calculate karke uska corresponding Hash Slot
determine karta hai.

Redis Cluster ki slot-to-node mapping ke basis par Redis Client identify
karta hai ki corresponding Hash Slot kis Redis Node ke paas assigned hai.

Is mapping ke basis par update request appropriate Redis Node tak route
ki jaati hai aur corresponding cache copy ko latest data ke saath update
kar diya jata hai.

Update ke baad:

product:101:1 → {iPhone, ₹120}
product:101:2 → {iPhone, ₹120}
product:101:3 → {iPhone, ₹120}


### PROBLEM
------------

Ek Database update ke baad sabhi N cache copies ko bhi update karna padta hai.

Example: N = 3 → 1 Database Update + 3 Cache Updates


### BENEFIT
------------
Agar sabhi cache copies successfully update ho jaati hain, to subsequent
read requests ke liye kisi bhi cache copy se immediately latest data
return kiya ja sakta hai.


### FAILURE SCENARIO
---------------------

Sabhi cache copies ka update successful hona zaroori hai.

Maan lo:

product:101:1 → Updated
product:101:2 → Updated
product:101:3 → Update Failed

Ab ek cache copy me purana data reh sakta hai.

Agar read request us stale copy ko select kare, to user ko stale data
mil sakta hai.

Isliye multiple cache copies ke saath consistency maintain karna
additional challenge hota hai.



